  "notes": "{途絶|とだ}える is an intransitive ichidan verb. It emphasizes that something which was ongoing or continuous has come to a stop.\n\nASPECT (ている):\n{途絶|とだ}えている describes a state where something has ceased and remains interrupted.\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {連絡|れんらく}が{途絶|とだ}える (communication is cut off)\n- {消息|しょうそく}が{途絶|とだ}える (whereabouts become unknown)\n- {伝統|でんとう}が{途絶|とだ}える (a tradition dies out)\n- {足音|あしおと}が{途絶|とだ}える (footsteps cease)\n- {供給|きょうきゅう}が{途絶|とだ}える (supply is cut off)\n\nOften conveys a sense of loss or concern. The transitive counterpart is {途絶|とだ}やす (to cut off, to let die out), though it is less common.",
